  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c78f3469548ae2b44bec77ce71d4f008, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::FunFactDatabase
  funFacts:
  - The decline of bee populations worldwide is a major concern for biodiversity
    and food security, as it threatens the pollination of many plants that are crucial
    for ecosystems and agriculture.
  - In addition to their ecological importance, bees also produce honey, which has
    been used by humans for thousands of years as a sweetener and for its medicinal
    properties.
  - Bees have a special structure called the "pollen basket" on their hind legs,
    which they use to collect and transport pollen back to the hive.
  - A single honeybee can visit up to 5,000 flowers in a day, and a hive of bees
    can pollinate millions of flowers in a season.
  - To produce just one pound of honey, a hive of bees has to embark on an incredible
    journey, traveling over 55,000 miles and visiting around two million flowers.
